# Bitcoin Lifeboat - Heir Recovery Plan (2-of-3)

_A plain-language plan for recovering a wallet that needs several devices._

```
You are reading a recovery plan written by someone who trusts you
to help. This document does not contain bitcoin. It does not contain
secret recovery words. It only contains instructions.

You will not be able to recover anything using only this document.
You will also need:
  - The wallet's hardware device(s) or backup card(s)
  - Any secret words the owner wrote down separately (if they did)
  - Patience: never let anyone rush you

If anyone tells you they can help you "decrypt" or "unlock" this
plan for a fee, hang up. They cannot. They are a scammer.

If you get stuck, stop. Take your time. Find a trusted person who
understands Bitcoin and ask for help. The owner has likely listed
a trusted helper on this page.
```

## Words to know

- Recovery words: the list of words (usually 12 or 24) the owner wrote down by hand. Sometimes called a seed phrase. They are the real key to the money. Never type them into a website.
- Hardware device: a small gadget that holds the recovery words offline and approves payments. Sometimes called a hardware wallet or a signing device.
- Recovery details: a long line of text (often starting with letters like "wpkh" or "wsh", and known to experts as a descriptor) that tells wallet software how to find this wallet. It is not a secret key, but keep it private.
- Wallet software: a free app (for example Sparrow or Bitcoin Core) that shows the wallet and helps approve a payment.
- Several-key wallet: a wallet that needs more than one device to approve a payment (experts call this multisig). You will need several of the devices listed in this plan.

## 1. Safety warning

Never type the recovery words into a website, an app, or a search box.

Bitcoin Lifeboat will never call you, message you, or ask for the recovery words. Anyone who does is trying to steal the money.

This document holds no secrets. The recovery words stay on paper or metal, never on a phone or computer.

## 2. Do not panic

If you are reading this during a hard time, slow down.

Recovering a wallet is a careful, step-by-step job. Most mistakes come from rushing. There is no deadline here.

Take a break before each big step. You can do this one piece at a time.

## 3. Stop and call a trusted helper

If any step is confusing, stop and ask a person you trust before you go on.

A trusted helper can be a family member, a friend, or an advisor who knows Bitcoin. The owner may have written one below.

Trusted helper to call: ______________________

Never let anyone watch you handle the recovery words, and never let anyone take a photo of them.

## 4. What you will need

- [ ] This plan, printed on paper.

- [ ] The written or stamped copy of the recovery details.

- [ ] Several of the devices or backups listed in section 7.

- [ ] A computer with the wallet software listed in section 10.

## 5. What kind of wallet this is

This page explains what kind of wallet you are recovering.

- This wallet needs 2 of 3 devices to approve a payment.

You must gather at least 2 of the 3 devices. If you can find 2, you can try to move the money. If too many are lost, the money cannot be moved.

## 6. The wallet's recovery details

The recovery details are like a map to the wallet. Without them, recovery is much harder and can be impossible.

Keep a written or stamped copy with each backup. The recovery details hold no secret words, but they reveal the wallet's history, so keep them private.

Recovery details: [descriptor redacted, see private export]

## 7. The devices and backups you need

Each device below helps approve a payment. You need enough of them to meet the number in section 5. Check that each one powers on.

- Signer A: short code 11111111

Signer A is kept at: ______________________

- Signer B: short code 22222222

Signer B is kept at: ______________________

- Signer C: short code 33333333

Signer C is kept at: ______________________

## 8. Is there an extra word (passphrase)?

The owner did not record an extra word (passphrase) for this wallet.

If you think there is one, stop. Do not go on without it. An extra word changes which wallet the recovery words reach.

## 9. People to contact

Fill these in by hand. Do not write the details of strangers or so-called recovery services here.

Trusted helper name: ______________________

Trusted helper phone: ______________________

Lawyer or executor: ______________________

## 10. The app you will need

You will need a free app on a computer to see the wallet and approve a payment.

- Sparrow

If that app stops working, the recovery details and recovery words still work with other Bitcoin apps that follow the same standard.

## 11. How to check the plan works

1. Open the wallet app and load the recovery details in WATCH-ONLY mode. Do not type any recovery words yet.

2. Check that the first address shown matches an address the owner left on record.

3. Gather at least 2 of the 3 devices and check you can approve a test payment with each.

4. Write today's date in section 13 once the practice run works.

## 12. When to stop right away

Stop and get help if any of these happen:

- A website, app, or person asks you to type the recovery words.

- An address does not match the one the owner left.

- Anyone pushes you to hurry, to share your screen, or to pay a fee.

- The app shows an amount or history you do not expect.

When in doubt, stop and call the trusted helper in section 9.

## 13. Date of the last practice run

Last successful practice run: 2026-05-01

Write the date here each time a practice run works.

## 14. When to practice next

Try again by: 2027-05-01

A practice run about once a year keeps the plan fresh. Sooner is better if a device or app has changed.

